# 014 — the /vendor/products/:id sub-routes now apply the same visibility gate
# as the detail route. Completes P0.3, which overlay 009 only half-closed.
#
# 009 guarded GET/POST/DELETE/cancel on `/vendor/products/:id`, but four sub-route
# matchers were left with no tenant check at all — `variants` (GET, POST),
# `variants/:variant_id` (GET, POST, DELETE — that one had `middlewares: []`) and
# `attributes/batch`. They use `seller_id` only as `created_by`. Reproduced live
# with rbac off: seller B posted to seller A's DRAFT product and got a staged
# `VARIANT_ADD` ProductChange attributed to B.
#
# The guard here is VISIBILITY, not ownership, and that distinction is the whole
# point. An existing upstream test — "allows any seller to request changes on a
# master product it did not create" — encodes the product decision: the shared
# catalogue is editable by any seller THROUGH THE REVIEW PIPELINE, and every one
# of these routes stages a reviewable ProductChange via
# productEditUpdateVariantsWorkflow rather than writing directly. Requiring
# ownership here breaks that test and the master-product model with it.
#
# Visibility closes the actual defect: another seller's DRAFT is not visible, so
# it 404s, while a published master product stays open to change requests.
#
# The parent routes keep `ensureSellerOwnsProductParam` from 009 — updating or
# deleting the product record itself is still owner-only.
#
# Live, rbac off, after: B -> A's draft 404 on all three; B -> published master
# GET 200 / attributes batch 202; A -> own product unaffected.
# http/product/vendor 19/19.

